**What to look at first:**
- **How I judged the earlier decisions:**
  - I agree with decisions 11 (R7), 12 (R1, R9, R14) and session 50 (R6).
  - I partly differ with session 53b (R17). Locality is not a gap in the proof. But Reviewer A's main objection is that "broad class" is meaningless, because one item holding the whole input makes any mapping fit. One sentence answers it.
  - Decision 12 has one gap. It explains the distance from Zainab et al.'s 83% evasion rate by an argument (their stricter false-positive rate and no byte cap), not by a run at their setting.
- **The investigate groups that turn on cheap checks:**
  - **baselines (R18):** a "trial table" built from the existing 73,063 catalogue edits marks a feature lowerable if some edit lowered it. Build it, then attack a monotone detector built on it. This is the competitor a practitioner who knows the twelve operations would actually use, and §5 never builds it. If it matches the derived table, the paper's contrast between computation and trial must be reframed.
  - **trust (R2):** two short checks. A multi-byte probe of the parser (write markers like MZ and Rich at every header position) would show whether the record of positions the parser reads is missing rules. A count of test files whose item kinds were never seen in the 2,000-file sample would show whether the sample-based part of the guarantee could be checked per file for free.
- **Two text fixes due regardless of any run:**
  - "such files are rare among benign software, and so stand out" (`_f_evaluation.tex:363`) contradicts the paper's own numbers and must go.
  - "The adaptive attack confirms the guarantee" (`_f_evaluation.tex:350`) should become "a check of the implementation", because a zero score drop follows from Corollary 1.
